     <section anchor="jsend" numbered="true" removeInRFC="false" toc="include" pn="section-appendix.a">
       <name slugifiedName="name-jsend">JSend</name>
       <t indent="0" pn="section-appendix.a-1">According to <xref target="JSend" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="JSend"/>:</t>
-      <t indent="0" pn="section-appendix.a-2"><artwork name="" type="" align="left" alt=""><![CDATA[
-   JSend is a specification that lays down some rules for how JSON
-   responses from web servers should be formatted.  JSend focuses on
-   application-level (as opposed to protocol- or transport-level)
-   messaging which makes it ideal for use in REST-style applications
-   and APIs.
-]]></artwork>
-</t>
+      <blockquote pn="section-appendix.a-2">
+        <t indent="0" pn="section-appendix.a-2.1">JSend is a specification that lays down some rules for how JSON
+responses from web servers should be formatted.  JSend focuses on
+application-level (as opposed to protocol- or transport-level)
+messaging which makes it ideal for use in REST-style applications
+and APIs.</t>
+      </blockquote>
       <t indent="0" pn="section-appendix.a-3">A basic JSend-compliant response must contain a &quot;status&quot; key and
 should contain &quot;data&quot;, &quot;message&quot;, and &quot;code&quot; keys dependent on the
 status value.  The following table lists the required and optional
 keys.</t>
